# Script files are hashed by their bytes (§5.12, §7 C3), and the documentation
# note beside each one records the digest it expects. With core.autocrlf on — as
# it is on the dev machine — git would hand a fresh clone CRLF line endings, the
# bytes would differ from the ones the hash was taken over, and "Check a
# script's file hash" would report a change nobody made.
#
# Pinning these to LF keeps the fixture's whole point intact: a match means the
# code is unchanged, and a difference means it really moved.
#
# Patterns here cannot contain spaces — git splits the line on whitespace — so
# these are repo-wide by extension rather than scoped to `test-vault/50 Scripts`.
# Nothing else in the repo carries these extensions.
*.R text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
